A||N||6||Monkeyman||brake dust shield thing||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||08-26-2000||08:16 AM||||Is the round metal part behind the front rotors the dust shield?  Exactly what is it's purpose?  Can it be safely removed?  I just swapped brakes (to GA) and the dust shield rubs the rotor from time to time.  Is there any reason I can't just remove it alltogether?<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000001||ourufo||08-26-2000||01:08 PM||||don't remove it, It helps keep water and road stuff of the braking surface. bent it or cut the small aria with tinsnips . Bending it back should work..<P>------------------<BR>OURUFOV8||||reg||12
Z||000002||Monkeyman||08-26-2000||01:17 PM||||I was afraid of that.  I bent part of it back, but it's still grinding.  I guess I'm just assuming that's what the problem is.  I was worried that I might not have gotten one of the bearings greased enough, but it doesn't pull, so unless I messed up both bearings equally, that doesn't seem likely.  I'm beginning to wonder if it is the dust shield, though.  The grinding sound (sounds sort of like sandpaper) comes and goes, it's never loud although the sound level does vary.  Seems like, if I use my brakes, the sound goes away until I let off of them.  It doesn't always come back right away.  Last symptom...I hear it more when I'm turning than when I'm in a straight line.<P>Any suggestions????? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||08-26-2000||01:53 PM||||I know of a couple people who make it a point to remove them.  What they gain you is almost negligible.  I wouldn't think twice about ditching mine.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||08-26-2000||02:16 PM||||It's there to prevent stones and such hitting the rotor and marking it up.<P>Are you sure it's the front ones..... You've had the whole thing apart recently. It could easily be a rear one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-26-2000||06:55 PM||||I hadn't thought about it being in the rear.  It sounded like it was coming from the left front, but it's hard to tell when your driving.  I have to pull all of the wheels off again to install the SS braided lines, so I'll try to check them all then.  Hopefully it's the dust shield, not something more important.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||08-26-2000||09:29 PM||||It's really easy to get the rear doing this. There is a certain mount of play built into the hub.<P>If you look at the shields you should be able to see where they hit the rotor. They usually hit right at the edge of the rotor.||||reg||1
